# The counted-day extracts are the tree's durable copy of a measurement that
# otherwise disappears. architecture/vllm.md §10's figures come from engine status
# lines that are byte-rotated (~17 h per 10 MB file at this load), so a counted day
# is unverifiable within a week unless its extract is committed — and the working
# copies have been landing in /tmp, which is tmpfs, where a reboot is what deletes
# them (#1812).
#
# The root .gitignore ends every new JSON in the tree (`*.json`), which is right for
# runtime data and wrong here: this is the directory whose whole job is to hold a
# fixture per counted day. The two extracts that predate #1812 got in only because
# someone force-added them past the rule, which is invisible to the next round and
# had already left the 09-28 reading uncommitted. The negation below is the visible
# version of that, scoped to the extract family rather than to all JSON, so a
# stray runtime JSON in this directory is still ignored — and so counting the NEXT
# day, which §10 owes, is one `git add` rather than a rule nobody can see.
#
# The root file itself is off-limits to the self-modification loop (denied in
# scripts/automod/spec.py), so a file like this one is the only route that does not
# end in a human typing `git add -f` for every counted day.
#
# The second family below is not a status-line extract: it is the frozen per-day
# fleet totals (turns / misses / re-prefill tokens / chat turns / session-kind mix)
# that §10's trigger-(ii) routine-exceedance claim rests on, read out of `usage.db`
# before the row-level churn the same database keeps makes the same claim
# un-re-derivable (#1919). It gets its own negation rather than a vllm_prefix_miss_*
# name because those bytes never came from a status line, and a fixture named for the
# wrong source is worse than one that is ignored.
!vllm_prefix_miss_*.json
!usage_day_totals_*.json

# The third family is not a counted day either: it is the `summary.overall.label_agreement`
# block of one nightly eval baseline, which #1938's tests re-derive their quoted figures
# from. Two things make the durable copy necessary rather than convenient. The full
# baseline is retention-swept (`eval/baselines` keeps 40 nightly runs), so the run this
# item's numbers came from is on a path that is scheduled to disappear; and the vault
# witness of the same block (`backlog/data/nightly-20260930-20260930-060339.json`) cannot
# be read from a test, because the gate runs with HOME at the round home where
# `~/obsidian` does not exist — a node that opened it would skip, and a skipping node
# pins nothing. Scoped to the label_agreement extract rather than to nightly baselines
# whole, so a real baseline JSON is still ignored: it carries gold quotes and query text.
!nightly-*-label_agreement.json

# The fourth family is a report this job wrote about itself. #2119's premise is one
# `stray_retention` block — a 1,250,205,696 B retired backup held from reclaim by a
# `-wal` 2.0 ms newer than its own main file, `planned` empty, `dry_run` false — and the
# item quotes numbers out of it. Its durable home is the vault witness under
# `backlog/data/` (clause 6), which a test cannot open for exactly the reason the
# paragraph above gives, and this family holds the same bytes again for that reason
# rather than as an extract: clause 6's own re-derive is `wc -l` of the whole report,
# and a block cut out of it cannot answer a line count. Byte-identical to the vault copy,
# md5 16647dfffb2d18a1a3ef84bd4059f91e, and
# `test_the_held_backup_witness_bytes_still_carry_the_measured_hold` re-derives every
# quoted figure from them. Scoped to this job's report family rather than to all JSON: a
# live report of this name belongs to `~/lloyd-data/_pipeline/reflection/`, never here,
# so the negation only ever reaches a witness somebody chose to commit.
!qmd-index-maintenance-*.json

# The fifth family is a session transcript's WITNESS — an extract, not the transcript.
# #2176's premise is that no instrument can say whether a shipped prompt-fix was in the
# prompt a live process assembled, and one of the three candidates for "there already is
# one" was the transcript itself. It is not: in
# `~/lloyd-data/sessions/20261004_023513_autotriage_33f5.json` (917 lines, 113,563 B, 39
# messages, no `system` role anywhere) `messages[0]` is the USER turn, 16,926 chars, and
# does not carry the paragraph `_data_home_hint` returns. `sessions/` is retention-swept
# the way the two families above are, and the vault copy under `backlog/data/` (clause 6)
# is unreadable from a node for the reason the paragraph above gives, so the extract lives
# here too. An extract rather than the whole file because the whole file is a triage
# session's prompt and reply text — the thing the manifest's retention policy exists not to
# retain — while every figure #2176 quotes is about messages[0] and is carried here with
# the source's digests beside it. `test_the_committed_witness_bytes_still_carry_the_measured_gap`
# re-derives them. Scoped to this name family so a transcript someone drops here by
# accident stays ignored.
!session_witness_*.json

# The sixth family is a database, and it is here for the same reason as the other five:
# #2168's premise is a measurement over `~/lloyd-data/usage.db`, a rolling store whose
# quoted window (2026-09-27T00:00:26 → 2026-10-04T08:17:44) slides out from under a reader
# within weeks, so the 271 clearing turns / 159 sessions / 112 repeat turns carrying
# 37,512,065 of 84,856,726 reprefilled tokens are unverifiable unless the rows behind them
# are committed. This copy is 4,852 rows over four columns (`ts`, `session_id`,
# `reprefill_tokens`, `compaction`), 4,112,384 bytes, two objects in `sqlite_master`;
# `test_the_witness_rows_behind_the_repeat_clear_measure_reproduce_the_quoted_report`
# re-derives the item's five figures from it. A copy is committed in the vault's witness
# store too, which is where clause 6 asked for it, under a name that does not collide with
# #1918/#1919's frozen extract already living at `backlog/data/usage.db`; a test cannot
# open either vault file, because the gate runs with HOME at the round home.
# Scoped to `usage-*.db` and not to `*.db`: the tree's runtime stores (`usage.db`,
# `workers.db`) are runtime data and stay ignored, and only a witness somebody chose to
# name this way lands here.
!usage-*.db

# The seventh family is one fact-improvement run record, committed for the reason every
# family above was: #2198's premise is a specific 2026-10-04 dry-run pass whose printed
# `signals=40` came entirely from drift while BOTH correction channels returned zero
# (`corrections_status: undated`; `lloyd/USER.md` at `entries: 2, undated: 2,
# signals: 0`; `memory/corrections.md` at `no_entries_in_window`, newest `2026-05-08`).
# The clause-5 re-derive is `wc -l` of the record WHOLE — 1052 lines — so a cut-down
# extract cannot answer the one question that clause asks, and the file needs history.
# Its durable home is the vault witness clause 5 names,
# `backlog/data/20261004-210010-dryrun.json` (committed under #2199), which a test
# cannot open: the gate runs with HOME at the round home, where `~/obsidian` does not
# exist. This copy is byte-identical to it, and the node named below compares the two
# whenever the vault is reachable instead of quoting a digest here — a bare object-id
# in a comment is read as a commit by whatever validates citations, and a compared
# byte is a better witness than a claimed one.
# `test_the_committed_witness_bytes_still_carry_the_quoted_two_silent_channels`
# re-derives every quoted figure, the 1052 lines included, from these bytes. Scoped to
# `improvement_*-dryrun.json` and not to all JSON: a live record of this shape belongs to
# `~/lloyd-data/_pipeline/improvement/` and is retention-swept, so this negation only
# ever reaches a witness somebody deliberately placed here.
!improvement_*-dryrun.json

# The eighth family is a seed, not a witness, and it is here for the opposite reason
# from the seven above: those preserve bytes that would otherwise be swept, this one
# authors bytes that never existed anywhere. #2202's measured pin cannot use the live
# backend as its denominator — "0 unreachable clipped labels" is also exactly what the
# dashboard says on a night when every task name is short — so the page it measures is
# fed a snapshot with seven deliberately long row labels and every list trimmed to a
# fixture-sized denominator. The file is read by `tests/test_dashboard_responsive.py`
# through `dashboard_mobile_probe.load_seed`, and the gate runs the suite in a worktree,
# where an ignored seed is simply absent: the pin would not skip, it would error, and a
# round that did not touch `web/` would look broken. Scoped to this one name rather than
# to `*.json` so a runtime snapshot dropped here by accident stays ignored.
!dashboard_long_labels.json

# The ninth family is the apply report behind #2255's premise, committed for the reason
# the seven witnesses above were: the clause-4 re-derive is `wc -l` of the report WHOLE
# (151 lines), and its durable home is the vault witness at
# `backlog/data/entity-merges-applied-2026-10-05-20261005T142239Z.json` (vault commit
# `d31bbbb7`), which a test cannot open because the gate runs with HOME at the round home,
# where `~/obsidian` does not exist. This copy is byte-identical to it —
# `test_the_witness_bytes_still_carry_the_gap_the_item_quotes` compares the two digests
# whenever the vault is reachable rather than quoting a digest as a fact — and re-derives
# the three clocks the item quotes from these bytes: the name's own `20261005T142239Z`, the
# `ledger.timestamp` 2026-10-05T21:22:40.345443+00:00 7 h 0 m 1 s later, and the
# `store_backup` name carrying the same naive stamp, plus the finding that a COMPLETE
# report carried no `timestamp` key at all. Scoped to this one filename, not to
# `entity-merges-applied-*.json`: a live report of that family belongs to
# `~/lloyd-data/_pipeline/memory-graph/` and is what the sweep is writing right now, so the
# negation must never reach one — only this witness somebody deliberately placed here.
!entity-merges-applied-2026-10-05-20261005T142239Z.json

# The tenth family is a measurement of the tree rather than a witness of an
# incident: the before/after capability table #2269's step-1 artifact is. It is
# committed because the acceptance is graded against it — "durable-external reach
# is 0 for every untrusted-ingest source, re-measure the digest baseline" is a
# claim about a number, and a reader who cannot see the number before the change
# cannot check the number after it. `test_the_step_1_artifact_is_committed_and_
# current` re-derives it from the compile on every run, so the committed copy
# cannot go stale against the code without the suite going red; it is committed
# rather than generated at test time so the diff of a round that widens or
# narrows a source is reviewable. Scoped to this one filename, not to `*.json`:
# a runtime dump dropped here by accident stays ignored.
!worker_capability_baseline.json
